## Why This Is Asked

This question probes how a candidate designs an actionable, beginner-friendly eval for grounding-heavy QA in a cross-brand setting (Tesla, MongoDB). It tests practicality, grounding fidelity, and the complete flow from data to metrics—not just theoretical understanding.

## Key Concepts

- **Grounding to versioned KB with provenance**: Each claim must reference a specific KB paragraph ID and version, enabling traceability
- **Hallucination taxonomy tailored for product support**: Fabrication (invented facts), misattribution (wrong brand/context), over-generalization (unverified sweeping claims)
- **Relevance to user intent**: Measuring whether responses actually address what users asked
- **Lightweight evaluation scripts**: Minimal, runnable code that beginners can extend

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple grounding verifier sketch
function groundClaim(claim, kb) {
  for (const p of kb.paragraphs) {
    if (claim.includes(p.key)) {
      return { grounded: true, paraId: p.id, version: p.version };
    }
  }
  return { grounded: false };
}

// Hallucination classifier
function classifyHallucination(claim, brand) {
  if (isInvented(claim)) return 'fabrication';
  if (wrongBrand(claim, brand)) return 'misattribution';
  if (isSweeping(claim)) return 'over-generalization';
  return 'valid';
}
```

## Why This Question Matters
Provides a practical, beginner-friendly approach to assessing grounding, code fidelity, and relevance for a developer assistant across private documentation corpora with versioning support.

## Key Concepts
- Grounding responses to exact doc version and section
- Detection of code block hallucinations
- Metrics: precision@1, grounding coverage, and citation drift

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal runner skeleton
const prompts = loadPrompts('prompts-50.json');
prompts.forEach(p => {
  const ans = model.ask(p);
  const grounded = verifyGrounding(ans, p.docVersion, p.section);
  const hallucinated = detectHallucinations(ans, p.docVersion);

  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    prompt: p.question,
    grounded,
    hallucinated,
    precisionAt1: computePrecision(ans, p.expectedEndpoints)
  }));
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical, end-to-end RAG pipeline with real-world constraints: chunking, embedding stability, vector DB tenancy, latency targets, and robust failover. It also probes how to evaluate recall/latency at scale across multiple tenants.

## Key Concepts

- Chunking strategy: 512-token blocks aligned to maintain context.
- Embeddings: stable models (SBERT or OpenAI Ada) with versioned updates.
- Vector DB tenancy: per-repo namespaces and strict access controls.
- Freshness: index aging, re-embedding on document updates.
- Latency: retrieval latency targets (p95/p50) and caching.
- Failover: lexical BM25 fallback and provenance surface.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: split text into 512-token chunks using a simple tokenizer (word-based placeholder)
function chunkDoc(text) {
  const tokens = text.split(/\s+/);
  const chunkSize = 512;
  const chunks = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tokens.length; i += chunkSize) {
    chunks.push(tokens.slice(i, i + chunkSize).join(' '));
  }
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure drift in embeddings over time and trigger re-embedding schedules?
- What tenancy/ACL considerations would you enforce for cross-repo queries?
- How would you structure A/B tests for recall vs latency trade-offs?

